{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CfpmQJPKzcr1ypW9WAZEq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Happy to think about this — and the task we just paused is a useful specimen, because it contains one of each.\n\n**They're orthogonal, not two ends of a scale.** \"Done\" asks whether the work satisfies the spec. \"Needs a human\" asks whether the spec *determines* the work. A task can be fully built and still be undetermined; it can be barely started and perfectly determined. The failure mode people actually hit is treating them as one axis — asking \"is it done?\" when the real question is \"was there ever a single right answer here?\"\n\n**The dangerous case is an underdetermined choice that gets silently resolved and then looks like doneness.** That's exactly the non-ASCII thing. The issue says \"non-alphanumeric.\" That phrase has two readings, I picked one, and if I'd just picked it without saying so, the test would have passed once I implemented it, the suite would be green, and the green would be *manufactured by my own guess*. The tests would be measuring their own self-consistency rather than anyone's intent. A passing suite tells you the code matches the tests; it tells you nothing about whether the tests match what someone wanted. Tests are only a doneness oracle to the degree that someone with authority over the intent has read them — which is why \"write the tests first and stop so I can look\" is structurally the right move. It puts the human at the exact moment ambiguity freezes into a contract.\n\nWorth noting *why* test-first catches this, since it's not the usual regression argument: you cannot write an assertion without committing to an exact value on the right side of the equals sign. \"Replace non-alphanumeric characters\" reads as complete prose right up until you have to type either `'caf-life'` or `'café-life'`. Prose hides underdetermination; assertions can't.\n\n**So the operative rule isn't ask-versus-decide — it's never let a resolution be invisible.** Three tiers, roughly:\n\n- Decide silently when the choice is genuinely unobservable and cheap to reverse (internal naming, loop structure).\n- Decide, proceed, and flag — this should be most cases. A stated assumption gets reviewed at the same moment as the code, with zero latency, and the person can veto it without having had to pre-answer a question.\n- Actually block only when proceeding under *any* assumption is unsafe or would waste the work if wrong.\n\nBlocking is overused. If I stop and ask every time something is ambiguous, I'm a bottleneck and you start skimming my questions — which costs you the one question that mattered.\n\n**The line I'd draw for \"genuinely human\" is whether the answer exists anywhere I can reach.** \"I don't know, but I could read the callers, check git history, or run it\" is not a judgment call, it's a lookup I haven't done, and escalating it burns credibility. \"I don't know, and the answer lives in someone's head or in a product decision nobody's made\" is the real thing. Null-input handling for `slugify` is the second kind: throw, coerce, return `''` are all defensible, there's no dominant convention, and the choice is visible in the API. Nothing in the repo settles it.\n\nThe sharpest proxy for weight, though, is usually reversibility — and notice that I often *can't* assess it from inside the task. Whether the ASCII choice matters depends on whether slug output ever becomes a URL that gets indexed or a database key. If so it's a one-way door and `'caf-life'` is permanent. If it's display-only it's trivially changed. The issue contains nothing about that. That asymmetry is a decent argument for flagging being cheap and default-on: I'm frequently not positioned to know which decisions are load-bearing.\n\n**On doneness specifically, the signal I'd trust has negative space in it.** Not \"tests pass\" but: every criterion has an observable check, the checks were actually run and watched rather than assumed, judgment calls are surfaced, *and* the behavior not covered by any criterion is named out loud. That last part is the valuable one — \"I did not test non-string input\" converts an unknown-unknown into a known-unknown, which is most of what honest status reporting buys you.\n\nAnd done is audience-relative. Done-for-review, done-for-merge, done-for-production are different thresholds. The slugify tests are done-for-review and nothing more, which is the state you asked for. Most \"is it done?\" disputes are really people holding different audiences in mind.\n\n**Where my own heuristic is weakest:** the \"would reasonable engineers diverge?\" test requires me to model other engineers, and I'll have blind spots — I can be confidently wrong that something is a universal default, which means the heuristic fails precisely where it's needed. And flag budget is real: ten flags per task means they get skimmed. Two, with one marked as the one that matters, is about the density I think holds up. I don't have a principled rule for that, just a suspicion that the ranking matters more than the count.\n\nOne thing I'd push back on if you proposed it: a checklist that tries to make doneness mechanical. The judgment-call detection is the irreducible part, and a checklist mostly moves the guessing into the checklist."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":790,"cache_read_input_tokens":21410,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":790},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b9dc2c99-fb6b-49bb-89ad-9c4b50a162c6","uuid":"3745ce51-9e0a-402a-8fe3-ff0d8eafe5a2","timestamp":"2026-10-08T12:26:56.235Z","request_id":"req_011CfpmQJ9hcPFHt2pudqVS8"}
